# [SPICE](../wg/spice.html)

## Summary

The SPICE Working Group met at IETF 121 to discuss progress on active working group items, charter updates, and new proposals. The charter objections have been resolved, formalizing the scope around interoperable credential profiles, selective disclosure, privacy/security guidelines, and registries for external label/identifier schemes.

Key updates included the publication readiness of OpenID Connect Claims for CWTs, solid progress on the architecture specification (`draft-ietf-spice-vdcarch`), and successful interop testing for SD-CWT. The working group also addressed blockates surrounding `draft-ietf-spice-glue-id` (GLUE) and evaluated a new extension proposal, SMD-CWT.

---

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group & Charter Status
* Slide Deck: [Chair slides](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-spice-chair-slides-01)
* The chairs confirmed that charter objections have cleared. 
* SPICE will focus on applying existing/emerging IETF technologies to digital credentials and selective disclosure, defining interoperable credential profiles, and establishing registries for external identifier schemes while coordinating closely with JOSE, COSE, OAuth, RATS, and CFRG.

### OpenID Connect Claims for CWTs
* Slide Deck: [OpenID Connect Claims for CWTs](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-spice-openid-connect-claims-for-cwts-00)
* **Presenter:** Mike Jones
* Publication was requested previously. AD Christopher Inacio noted feedback regarding certain data structure uses. The authors will address these structural feedback items in an updated draft before progressing to the IESG.

### Architecture Draft
* Draft: `draft-ietf-spice-vdcarch`
* Slide Deck: [SPICE Architecture](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-spice-spice-architecture-00)
* **Presenter:** Brent Zundel
* The document structure has been updated to align with standard IETF architecture formats, incorporating sections for use cases, terminology, general architecture, actor constraints, trust models, and core interaction flows.
* **Flows & Roles:** Rohan Verma suggested adding a flow without a mediator to simplify the baseline cases. 
* **Use Cases:** Henk Birkholz suggested adding 3–5 exemplary use cases directly in the architecture document rather than pointing to an expired draft. Henk committed to delivering text for this by December.
* **Component Terminology:** 
  * Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) may be mentioned where appropriate, but "trusted displays" will be omitted as out of scope.
  * Credential Manager and Presentation Mediator will remain distinct roles/tools in the architecture, even if combined in basic implementation deployments.
  * The group agreed to retain "Credential Manager" as the formal technical term while describing "wallets" as a colloquial concept or use case.

### SD-CWT Update
* Slide Deck: [SD-CWT Update](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-spice-sd-cwt-update-00)
* **Presenter:** Rohan Verma
* The draft has been submitted to the AD for publication. 
* Successful interop testing was conducted across three implementations (Python, JavaScript, and Rust) with 40 out of 40 test cases passing across 738 total checks.
* Recent updates (-08) moved the `SD-AEAD` header to the unprotected header parameters (since it is set by the holder) and corrected byte string encodings in the specification examples.
* In response to a query from Brent Zundel regarding ISO mDoc comparisons, Rohan Verma affirmed that SD-CWT is feature-complete, fully interoperable, and ready for publication on its own merits.

### GLUE (GLobal Unique Enterprise Identifiers)
* Draft: `draft-ietf-spice-glue-id`
* Slide Deck: [GLUE](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-spice-glue-00)
* **Presenter:** Orie Steele
* The draft was blocked due to designated expert objections regarding URI and URN scheme registrations and authority allocation.
* Justin Richer, Mike Jones, and Daniel Gillmor suggested abandoning URI/URN scheme syntax in favor of a string format with a prefix (e.g., using `glue_` or string prefixes).
* Carsten Bormann questioned the necessity of string representations over a structured data model (such as CBOR), while Mike Jones emphasized the practical necessity of string encodings when handling existing organizational identifiers in protocols.
* **Formal Sessions Polls:**
  * **Poll 1:** "GLUE" — yes: 0, no: 0, no_opinion: 0 (total: 33)
  * **Poll 2:** "Should we continue work on GLUE?" — yes: 6, no: 4, no_opinion: 7 (total: 33)
  * **Poll 3:** "Are people who voted “yes” good with a string prefix?" — yes: 5, no: 1, no_opinion: 2 (total: 32)
* Based on Poll 3, the working group decided to unstick `draft-ietf-spice-glue-id` by pivoting to a string prefix representation.

### SMD-CWT: Merkle Tree Selective Disclosure
* Slide Deck: [SMD-CWT](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-spice-smd-cwt-01)
* **Presenter:** Bertrand Maldant
* Bertrand presented a proposal for extending SD-CWT using Merkle trees (SMD-CWT) to achieve logarithmic inclusion proof sizes instead of linear hash enumerations, significantly reducing token size for large claim sets and enhancing privacy against array size leakage.
* **Discussion:**
  * Daniel Gillmor inquired about zero-knowledge proof (ZKP) applicability; Bertrand clarified that SMD-CWT targets simple, compact selective disclosures where linkability across presentations is acceptable or intended.
  * Rohan Verma asked about encryption overhead; Bertrand noted that multiple inclusion proofs can be bundled under a single AEAD encryption wrapper.
  * Justin Richer asked whether this mechanism could extend to JSON/JOSE; Bertrand confirmed the mathematical structure is format-agnostic.

### Other Business (MOSIP Specification)
* Mike Jones raised a question regarding whether MOSIP’s CWT identity claims specification (claim 169) could be standardized within SPICE.
* Chairs and AD Christopher Inacio clarified that bringing specifications into an IETF working group requires transferring full change control to the IETF. If MOSIP prefers retaining change control, the Independent Submission Stream (ISE) or AD sponsorship would be more suitable paths.

